## Why This Is Asked
Explores runtime policy update, data residency, and per-tenant isolation without redeploying models. 

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy engine integrated with config store
- Per-tenant data sovereignty and RBAC
- Latency targets and fallback paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: policy fetch & eval before OpenAI call
async function handle(req, tenantId){
  const policies = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!evaluate(policies, req)) throw new Error('Policy violation');
  const redacted = redact(req.body);
  return await callOpenAI(redacted);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test runtime policy changes with live traffic?
- How do you roll back a faulty policy update?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests CDS governance, real-world guardrails, and Azure AI integration.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant prompts
- PII redaction and safety checks
- Governance metadata and provenance
- Audit logging to Data Lake
- Fallback policies and test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// example wrapper pseudocode for gating
async function respond(input, tenantId){
  const redacted = redactPII(input);
  const ctx = await fetchContext(tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(ctx, redacted);
  const risk = await safetyCheck(prompt);
  if(!risk.ok) return fallbackResponse(ctx);
  const res = await openaiChat(prompt);
  const final = attachGovernance(res, tenantId, ctx.modelVer);
  await log(final);
  return final;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the end-to-end logging and searchability of prompts? 
- How would you handle model version rollouts with zero-downtime for tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design secure, scalable, multi-tenant AI-assisted incident triage on Azure with data governance.

## Key Concepts

- Retrieval-augmented generation
- Multi-tenant RBAC and data isolation
- PII redaction and compliance
- Canary deployment and rollback
- Audit logs and data lake

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch of flow stages
async function handleEvent(e) {
  const norm = normalize(e);
  const doc = await kbQuery(norm, tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(doc, norm);
  const resp = await openai.infer(prompt);
  const safe = redact(resp);
  await logAudit({tenantId, event: e, resp: safe});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the sandboxing to prevent prompt injection in a CI/CD pipeline?
- Which metrics and SLAs would you monitor to ensure per-tenant quota enforcement doesn't throttle critical alerts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multilingual, policy-respecting flows with Azure AI services and end-to-end data handling. Emphasizes privacy, per-tenant configuration, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI, API Management, Cognitive Search, Blob
- Azure Translator, Text Analytics for redaction
- Cosmos DB for tenant config, Data Lake for audit logs
- End-to-end data flow and test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// PII redaction and translation flow (pseudo)
async function prepareQuery(input, targetLang){
  const redacted = redactPII(input);
  const translated = await translateText(redacted, targetLang);
  const prompt = `Q: ${translated}\nA:`
  return prompt;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test translation accuracy across languages?
- How would you handle translation errors impacting latency?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes designing a scalable, secure, multi-tenant pipeline that integrates Azure OpenAI with per-tenant governance, data redaction, and auditable logs. It emphasizes data flow clarity and practical constraints in production.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Event Hubs, Function/Stream Analytics pipelines
- Azure OpenAI with per-tenant prompts
- Cognitive Search KB grounding against Blob storage
- PII/PHI redaction with Text Analytics
- Data Lake per-tenant logs and auditability
- Azure AD RBAC and API Management canaries

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: tenant-scoped routing
async function routeTelemetry(event) {
  const tenant = extractTenant(event);
  const normalized = normalize(event);
  const prompt = buildPromptForTenant(tenant, normalized);
  const res = await openai.createChatCompletion({
    model: 'gpt-4o',
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: prompt }],
  });
  await redactAndStore(tenant, event, res);
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate latency isolation per tenant at high ingestion rates?
- What strategies ensure prompt drift is detected and rolled back without downtime?
